# Git Branching Strategy

Rarely, if ever, push code directly to `main`. Use structured branches to keep production stable.

## Branches

| Branch | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| `main` | Sacred. Stable, deployed, or deployable code only. |
| `develop` | Integration/staging — features are merged and tested here before `main`. |
| `feature/feature-name` | New work. Branch off `develop` (or `main` only for hotfix-only cases). Example: `feature/user-authentication`. |
| `bugfix/bug-description` | Targeted fixes. Example: `bugfix/login-crash`. |

## Workflow

1. Create a branch from `develop` for features or bug fixes.
2. Open a PR into `develop` when work is ready for integration testing.
3. Merge `develop` into `main` only when the release is stable and deployable.
4. Do not commit or push directly to `main` unless the user explicitly requests an emergency hotfix.

## Agent behavior

- Before starting work, confirm the current branch is not `main`.
- Use descriptive branch names: `feature/...` for features, `bugfix/...` for fixes.
- When creating PRs, target `develop` by default (not `main`).
- Only push to `main` when the user explicitly asks.
